// DEM dry-run validation: static-pile angle of repose (elongated cylinders, poured from a point source
// into a small bounded pit -- see DEMValidationScenarios.h's calibration notes for why a fully open
// floor and a sphere/cylinder mix were both tried and abandoned) checked against a broad granular
// plausibility band, anchored by Kodam et al. (2010) Part II's own cylinder result (39.6deg simulated
// vs. 43.9deg experimental, aspect ratio ~0.66).
//
// CURRENT STATUS: FAILS (reposeAngle ~1deg, i.e. flat) even after fixing every setup bug found along the
// way (numerically-safe non-overlapping placement, sane pour-column height, bounded containment). The
// root cause is now a genuine, diagnosed CONTACT-MODEL limitation, not a test bug: mesa_pd's
// LinearSpringDashpot implements pure Coulomb sliding friction with no explicit rolling-resistance
// torque, so particles roll/slide out to a flat layer rather than mounding up, regardless of shape or
// containment -- a well-known real gap in plain-Coulomb DEM codes (granular DEM calibration literature
// routinely needs an added rolling-friction term for a realistic angle of repose). This is a legitimate
// finding this validation surfaced about the DEM implementation, worth reporting as such rather than
// tuned away. A rotating-drum variant closer to Kodam's own Test 4 setup would need new moving-boundary
// infrastructure this app doesn't otherwise require, and would not fix the underlying gap either.
DEMValidation{
    testCase angleOfRepose;

    // Kept modest deliberately: DEMValidationScenarios.h's runAngleOfRepose pours particles from a
    // stacked column that grows with numParticles, and a taller column means a higher free-fall impact
    // speed on landing -- too many particles here reintroduces the "poured pile scatters instead of
    // piling up" failure mode documented in that function's own calibration note.
    numParticles 60;
    sphereRadius 1.0;
    cylinderRadius 0.8;
    // Aspect ratio 4 (rod-like), not 2 (disk-like): mesa_pd's contact law has no explicit rolling-
    // resistance torque, so a short, disk-like cylinder can roll like a wheel almost as freely as a
    // sphere; an elongated rod resists end-over-end rolling far better, which matters here because
    // useCylinders=true makes every particle a cylinder (see DEMValidationScenarios.h's comment on why).
    cylinderLength 3.2;
    useCylinders true;
    density 1.0;

    restitutionCoefficient 0.3;
    frictionCoefficient 0.6;
    poissonsRatio 0.22;
    collisionTime 0.01;
    gravity 9.81;

    // 15 (30x30 floor), not 30 (60x60): 150 particles poured from a small point source form a modest
    // pile footprint; a needlessly large floor only means a much bigger (much slower) LinkedCells grid
    // every step for no physical benefit.
    floorHalfExtent 15.0;

    maxSteps 60000;
    vtkSpacing 500;
    outputBaseName angle_of_repose;
}
